**Current support versus accepted design.** Directory commands now select one
directory as both build root and source root. `config.moth` is optional and its
`project` record may omit `name` and `version`. Generated outputs default to
`_dev` and `_release`. The accepted `$config`, `$project` and `$html_builder`
forms are queued, and `moth new` does not emit them yet. A general `$entry` root
purpose is deferred; the earlier `$page` proposal is superseded.

A Moth project is a directory tree rooted at the directory selected by the
command. There is no implicit `src/` entry directory, and a nested
`config.moth` never applies to an enclosing build.

### Accepted starter shape

The queued directive form leaves version optional:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $project(
        name = "my_site",
    )

    $html_builder
]

This is accepted queued design, not a claim about the current `moth new`
scaffold. Keep operational projects on the current declaration-owned
`#Config` and grouped parenthesised config until the general-directive
cutover. `$project` never accepts `entry_root`.

### Project root contents

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    website/
    ├── config.moth           # optional
    ├── @site.moth
    ├── shared/
    │   └── +package.moth
    ├── pages/
    │   └── @pages.moth
    ├── _dev/
    │   └── .moth_manifest
    ├── _release/
    │   └── .moth_manifest
    └── .gitignore
]

### Directory roles

- The selected directory is the only build root and the top of the discoverable source tree.
- `config.moth` is optional. When present, only the one at the selected root applies. It is not module source.
- A directory named `src/` is ordinary source content, not a configured entry root. There is no `entry_root`.
- `_dev/` and `_release/` are hidden generated output folders, not source folders. The build system manages them through manifests.
- `.moth_manifest` files are build-system metadata used for safe stale-output cleanup. They are not source.
- A directory component below the selected root whose name starts with `_` is excluded from that build. Select it directly to compile it.

### Structural packages

Project-local source-backed packages are structural `+*.moth` support packages.
A `+*.moth` file in a source directory marks that directory as a scoped
support package root named by its containing directory.

In the example above, `shared/+package.moth` creates the scoped `@shared`
support package. It is visible to its owner normal module, normal sibling
modules and their descendants. It is not dependency-bound from its own private
implementation subtree or from another support package in the same scope.

No folder gains package meaning without a `+*.moth` root. Ordinary directories
do not automatically become packages.

A directory may contain both an `@*.moth` normal root and a `+*.moth` support
root. They are two isolated surfaces of one structural module: helpers are
shared, roots cannot import each other, and both interfaces publish only from
one successful semantic transaction. A project-root `+*.moth` is the ordinary
support surface selected by project package assembly; there is no separate
facade node or special assembly namespace.

For the full package contract see @../packages/#project-local-packages (Project-local packages).

### Normal module-root files inside source directories

A non-config file whose name starts with `@` and ends with `.moth` is a module
root. The filename after `@` has no language-level semantic role.
`@page.moth`, `@mod.moth`, `@api.moth` and `@anything.moth` are equivalent root
filenames.

The current `moth new html` scaffold uses `@page.moth` at the selected root as a
conventional root filename. That convention does not make the filename a page.
Today the root becomes an HTML entry from authored root activity. A direct
builder slot contribution is const-fragment activity:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[$in title:Welcome]
]

A future general `$entry` purpose will make entry selection explicit. It is
deferred and is not current syntax; the earlier `$page` proposal is superseded.

Helpers in ordinary files stay inside the surrounding module and cannot become
entries on their own.

### Current scaffold notice

The current scaffold creates current-syntax `config.moth` with an explicit valid
project name, a root-level `@page.moth`, and a `.gitignore` containing `/_dev`
and `/_release`. The generated config is:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    project #= (
        name = "my_site",
    )
    html #= ()
]

It does not emit the accepted starter `$config`/`$project`/`$html_builder` shape
or an explicit `$entry` declaration. It does emit direct builder slot
contributions in `@page.moth`. Deleting the generated config is valid when the
directory name is a valid project identifier.

### Source versus output

Developers own source directories. The builder generates hidden output folders
and owns their stale-output cleanup. Treat generated artefacts as output, not
source. A visible directory named `release` remains ordinary source.

### Builder-neutral frontend versus builder-owned output

The compiler frontend discovers modules, resolves dependencies, checks types,
lowers code and validates borrowing. A project builder then decides how
compiled modules become real output files. The current user-facing builder is
the HTML project builder.

This separation lets the same frontend feed other builders. The frontend does
not decide which modules produce artefacts. The builder owns that decision.

### Only config.moth has config semantics

No other filename has config meaning. `@config.moth` is a module root, not a
config file. Alternate config filenames, compatibility handling and
config-specific diagnostics for other names are not supported. A nested
`config.moth` is reserved and inert to the enclosing build.

### Concepts related to project layout

- For config key rules see @#project-config (Project config).
- For module root rules see @#module-roots (Module roots).
- For HTML routes and artefacts see @#html-routing-and-artifacts (HTML routing and artefacts).
